---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro'
import { site } from '../config/site'
import '../styles/doc.css'
---

<!-- Explains the ladder in structural terms only. Entry fees, prizes, XP
     values and unlock levels are operator-tuned in table_configs /
     level_configs, so exact numbers stay in the app and out of this page. -->
<LegalLayout
  title={`Ranks and Stakes — How the Ladder Works in ${site.name}`}
  description={`How tiers, entry stakes, prizes, XP, levels, and PvP rating work in ${site.name}.`}
  canonical={`${site.url}/ranks-and-stakes`}
>
  <h1>Ranks and Stakes</h1>
  <p class="doc-meta">
    What you pay to sit down, what you win, and how the game measures you. All
    figures are in Coins, which are virtual: {site.disclaimer}
  </p>

  <h2>Five tiers</h2>
  <p>
    Tables run from <strong>Beginner</strong> through <strong>Advanced</strong>,
    <strong>Pro</strong> and <strong>Expert</strong> to
    <strong>Grand Master</strong>. Each tier sets four things:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the entry stake in Coins;</li>
    <li>the prize for winning and the consolation for losing;</li>
    <li>the XP you earn, which rises with the tier; and</li>
    <li>the time you get to make a move.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Higher tiers are also gated by your level, so the expensive tables open as
    you gain experience rather than the moment you can afford one loss. The exact
    numbers are shown on the tier cards in the app and are tuned over time — we
    do not publish a table here that would go stale.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens to the stake</h2>
  <p>
    Both players pay the entry stake up front. Against another player, the two
    stakes form the pot: the winner takes the pot minus a small house cut and
    minus the loser's consolation. Against an AI opponent, the prize is a fixed
    amount for the tier, so a match against a bot pays the same whether or not
    another human was available.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your first few difficulty matches against an AI opponent are risk-free: if
    you lose, the entry stake comes back. It is there so a new player can try a
    tier without being emptied by variance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Abandoning a match forfeits the coins but still counts as a loss for your
    rating. Sitting out a bad game is not a free exit.
  </p>

  <h2>XP, levels, and status</h2>
  <p>
    Every finished match pays XP, scaled by the tier. XP fills your level;
    crossing a level pays out Coins or Gems automatically and can unlock the
    next tier or lobby feature. Your status label — the title next to your name
    — is derived from your level, so it moves with the same progress bar rather
    than being a separate currency.
  </p>

  <h2>PvP rating</h2>
  <p>
    Matches against real players also move an Elo rating, shown on your
    profile. Everyone starts
    at 1500. You gain rating for beating a stronger opponent and lose more for
    losing to a weaker one, exactly as in chess. Matchmaking prefers opponents
    inside a rating band around you, so the ladder finds your level rather than
    throwing you at the strongest player online.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rating is not the same as level. Level measures how much you have played;
    rating measures how well.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the house takes a cut</h2>
  <p>
    In player-versus-player matches a small percentage of the pot is removed
    before the prize is paid. That cut is what keeps the Coin economy from
    inflating: Coins enter the game constantly through bonuses, missions, and
    the wheel, and something has to take them back out. It is a balancing
    mechanism inside a virtual currency, not a fee you pay in money.
  </p>

  <h2>Running out of Coins</h2>
  <p>
    You are never stuck. The hourly wheel, the daily bonus, and daily missions
    all pay Coins for free — see <a href="/rewards">Rewards</a>. Buying Coins is
    optional and never buys better dice; see <a href="/fair-play">Fair Play</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
